topic.install.body:
Phoenix MCP is a Home Assistant integration. Install it, add it once, and its panel appears in the sidebar.

## Requirements {#requirements}

Home Assistant 2026.7.0 or newer. Phoenix needs nothing else installed: its safety layer, MESA, comes with it.

## Installing from the Marketplace {#marketplace}

From Home Assistant 2026.11, go to Settings, Marketplace, and choose Add from link. Enter `https://github.com/leecaochang/Phoenix-MCP`, then choose Add. The Marketplace asks you to connect GitHub the first time. Open Phoenix MCP and choose Install. Restart Home Assistant only if it asks you to.

If you upgrade from HACS, Phoenix moves to the Marketplace by itself. Connect GitHub there to keep getting updates.

## Installing with HACS {#hacs}

On Home Assistant 2026.10 or older, add `https://github.com/leecaochang/Phoenix-MCP` to HACS as a custom repository of type Integration. Download Phoenix MCP, then restart Home Assistant. HACS checks the Home Assistant version for you.

## Installing by hand {#manual}

Copy the `phoenix_mcp` folder into your Home Assistant's `custom_components` folder, then restart Home Assistant. On an older Home Assistant, Phoenix doesn't start, and the Home Assistant log says which version it needs.

## Adding the integration {#setup}

In Home Assistant, go to Settings, Devices & services, then Add integration, and choose Phoenix MCP. There's nothing to fill in. You can add it only once.

## The panel {#panel}

**Phoenix MCP** appears in the sidebar for administrators only. Everything else is set up from there, starting with an access key.

Each access key also gets a device in Home Assistant, with sensors for its status, requests, refusals, rate limit hits, last use and expiry. Agents can't see these sensors.

## Language {#language}

The panel speaks 16 languages. By default it follows your Home Assistant profile; choose another in [{{label:settings.languageLabel}}](guide:window.settingsGeneral#language). The choice applies to this browser only. The Guide shows each page in your language when it's translated, and in English otherwise.

## Removing Phoenix MCP {#removing}

Deleting the integration revokes every access key, cancels requests waiting for approval, and erases every AI provider account and its API key. It also unlinks Phoenix from Assist, the voice agent, AI Task and the decision provider.

Everything else stays in Home Assistant's storage: settings such as the kill switch and MESA mode, the audit log, Conversation Log, MESA profiles, change history and recovery copies. If you add Phoenix again, it picks them up, and the revoked keys are listed in Archived Access Keys.

To remove Phoenix's data too, use **{{label:settings.wipeCoreLabel}}** in [{{label:settings.wipeTitle}}](guide:window.settingsSafety#wipe-core) before deleting the integration. [A few things stay](guide:topic.security#removing), including safety copies.

topic.passThrough.body:
A pass-through key skips the Permissions Tree: it can read and control every entity, except Phoenix's own. Use it only for an agent you trust with your whole home.

## What it turns on {#allows}

Pass-through also turns on these capabilities. They stay on while it's on, and none of them can be Confirm or Deny:

- {{label:caps.cap_config_read.label}}, {{label:caps.cap_template_render.label}}, {{label:caps.cap_search.label}} and {{label:caps.cap_registry_read.label}}.
- {{label:caps.cap_traces.label}}, {{label:caps.cap_diagnostics.label}}, {{label:caps.cap_broadcast.label}} and {{label:caps.cap_service_response.label}}.

{{label:caps.cap_diagnostics.label}} and {{label:caps.cap_service_response.label}} can expose sensitive details about your system.

## What stays in your control {#keeps}

Every other capability keeps its own setting. That includes physical control, restarts, notifications, camera images, system logs, files, ESPHome YAML and every configuration change. Confirm stays Confirm.

Locks, alarm panels, covers and valves still need {{label:caps.cap_physical_control.label}}. Sensitive attributes, such as camera stream addresses, are still removed.

## Narrowing it {#limit}

**{{label:accessKeys.assistExposureLabel}}** in [{{label:workspace.keySettings}}](guide:window.keySettings#assist-exposure) limits the key to the entities exposed to Assist. Home Companion keys can't use pass-through at all.

## Turning it off {#off}

**{{label:accessKeys.switchToScoped}}** in the [{{label:accessKeys.permissionsTree}}](guide:window.permissions#pass-through) window. The key uses its saved tree again.

topic.permissionResolution.body:
Phoenix decides every request the same way, whichever app or feature sends it.

## The order {#order}

1. An entity that doesn't exist is NOT FOUND.
2. Phoenix's own entities are never reachable.
3. A Home Companion key reaches only its household devices.
4. A pass-through key reaches everything else, or only Assist-exposed entities if you limited it.
5. Otherwise Phoenix reads the tree: the entity, its device, the main device it's part of (if any), then its domain.

## Deny wins {#deny}

A D anywhere in that chain denies, even when the entity itself is set to W.

A group with a D member is refused too, because acting on the group would reach that member. In a wider request, such as a whole area, Phoenix leaves the group out and acts on the rest. A Switch as X entity counts as a group holding the switch behind it. Scenes, scripts and automations don't count as groups.

## The nearest grant decides {#nearest}

Otherwise the nearest R or W decides. An entity's own setting beats its device's, and a device's beats its domain's. If every level is N, the answer is NO ACCESS.

## Examples {#examples}

### Read every light, control one room {#example-one-room}

Set the light domain to R and the living room light to W. Every light is READ, and the living room light is WRITE, because its own setting is nearest.

### Control every light except one bedroom {#example-except}

Set the light domain to W and the guest bedroom's device to D. Every other light is WRITE, and the bedroom's lights are DENY.

### Block one sensor on a device you allow {#example-one-sensor}

Set the media player's device to W and its signal strength sensor to D. The agent controls the player but can't see that sensor.

## Assist and the Voice Agent {#household}

When someone speaks to Assist or the Voice Agent, Phoenix also limits the result to what that Home Assistant user may see and control.

Related: [Capabilities for household speakers](guide:topic.tools#household)

## Disabled entities {#registry-only}

An entity that's disabled, or has no current state, is visible only through a grant on its device or domain.

## What the tree shows {#tree-label}

The result after each name in the tree follows the tree. On a Home Companion key it also shows the companion's limits: devices it can't reach show No Access, and Write on a sensor shows Read. It doesn't take the person speaking into account.

## Control through automations, scripts and scenes {#indirect}

An automation, script or scene runs as Home Assistant, not as the agent. So W on one lets an agent trigger actions its own key can't reach. A key with D on a door lock can still unlock it by running an automation that does.

R is safe for triggering: the agent can see these entities but can't run them. The tree marks these domains with "!".

To guard a trigger, give the automation, script or scene a MESA profile set to Confirm. When {{label:settings.mesaModeLabel}} or that profile is {{label:settings.mesaModeEnforced}}, each run waits for your approval, whatever its actions do. Under {{label:settings.mesaModeAdvisory}}, the run goes ahead. [{{label:mesa.suggestTitle}}](guide:window.mesaSuggestions) finds the ones that reach risky devices.

An approval covers the exact entities, and any group members, it was requested for. If the group changes, the agent must ask again. Phoenix doesn't check what a scene or script then does.

An entity the agent names in a run's inputs is different: it [needs the same access as a target](guide:topic.tools#actions). Phoenix sees only what an input names outright, so a script that builds an entity ID from parts gets past this.

Generic Thermostat and Generic Hygrostat helpers also act as Home Assistant. Phoenix doesn't look through them to the switch they drive.

Related: [Automations and scripts](guide:topic.tools#automations)

## Always blocked {#always-blocked}

Whatever the tree says, and with pass-through too, no key can reach Phoenix's own entities, such as its access key sensors. Secrets in attributes are removed as well.

Related: [What agents never see](guide:topic.security#hidden)

topic.welcome.body:
The Guide explains Phoenix as you work. Try any of these, or just carry on.

## Point at a button {#point}

**{{label:help.title}}**, along the bottom, says what it does. Show or hide it in the **{{label:shell.help}}** menu.

## Select something in a window {#select}

The Guide finds the page about it.

## Search any keyword or topic {#search}

## Customize appearance {#look}

Also in **{{label:shell.tabSettings}}**, under **{{label:workspace.general}}**.

window.accessKeys.body:
Your access keys. Select a key to show its settings in the other windows. Until you select one, only this list is shown.

## The list {#key-list}

Each row shows the key's name, mode and status, and when it was created, last changed, expires and was last used. Revoked and expired keys move to [{{label:accessKeys.archivedTitle}}](guide:window.archivedAccessKeys).

Type in the filter box above the list to find a key by name or status. You can sort, move, resize and hide columns; the layout is kept in this browser.

The Mode column shows Scoped, or {{label:accessKeys.passThrough}} for a key that skips the permissions tree.

Related: [What pass-through opens](guide:topic.passThrough)

## {{label:accessKeys.createAccessKey}} {#create-key}

**{{label:accessKeys.createAccessKey}}** on the toolbar opens a short form above the list. Select it again to close the form.

### {{label:accessKeys.nameLabel}} {#key-name}

3 to 32 letters, digits, underscores or hyphens. Each name must be unique, ignoring case, and a hyphen counts the same as an underscore. The name appears in the audit log and in approvals, so say which agent uses the key.

### {{label:accessKeys.expiryLabel}} {#key-expiry}

No Expiry (default), or a number of minutes, hours, days or weeks. When the key expires, Phoenix archives it. You can't change the expiry after the key is created.

### Rate limits {#key-rate-limits}

**{{label:accessKeys.rateRequestsLabel}}** (default 60) and **{{label:accessKeys.rateBurstLabel}}** (default 10). You can change both later.

Related: [Rate limiting](guide:window.keySettings#rate-limiting)

### {{label:accessKeys.passThroughMode}} {#key-pass-through}

Makes the key skip the permissions tree, so it can reach every entity. Off by default. Turn on the acknowledgement before you create it.

Related: [What pass-through opens](guide:topic.passThrough)

### After you create the key {#key-created}

Phoenix asks how you'll use the new key:

- **{{label:accessKeys.postCreateConnectTitle}}**: setup steps for your app, with the key filled in.
- **{{label:accessKeys.postCreateChatTitle}}**: opens Settings, to use the key with Agent Chat, the Voice Agent or AI Task. The key's value isn't shown.
- **{{label:accessKeys.postCreateShowTitle}}**: the key's value, to copy.

Copy the key before you close this dialog. Phoenix stores only a hash of it and can't show it again. If you lose it, rotate the key.

## {{label:workspace.keyActions}} {#key-actions}

**{{label:workspace.keyActions}}** on the toolbar acts on the selected key. Every action except Rename asks you to confirm, above the list.

### {{label:workspace.rename}} {#rename}

Turns the key's name into a text box. Press Enter to save, or Esc to cancel. You can also double-click the name. The usual name rules apply.

### {{label:accessKeys.rotate}} {#rotate}

Gives the key a new secret value and shows it. Everything else stays the same, including its permissions and history. The old value stops working straight away, so update every app that uses it. **{{label:accessKeys.helpConnect}}** in the same dialog shows each app's setup with the new value.

### {{label:accessKeys.enablePtLabel}} {#enable-pass-through}

Makes the key skip the permissions tree. The tree is kept: **{{label:accessKeys.switchToScoped}}** in the {{label:accessKeys.permissionsTree}} window turns it back on. Home Companion keys can't use pass-through.

Related: [What pass-through opens](guide:topic.passThrough)

### {{label:accessKeys.revoke}} {#revoke}

Ends the key for good. Its requests are refused, and its waiting approvals are cancelled. Phoenix also removes it from the Voice Agent, AI Task and Assist Tool Provider, and moves it to {{label:accessKeys.archivedTitle}}. You can't undo this.

## Reconnecting an agent {#reconnect}

When you change which tools a key may use, a banner above the list asks you to reconnect its agent. Some apps only read their list of tools when they connect. The banner appears only for keys that have been used.

Giving a key its first Write in the permissions tree, or removing its last one, changes its tools too, and shows the banner as well.

window.approvals.body:
The requests agents made. Choose one to see it in [{{label:workspace.review}}](guide:window.review). The list updates itself, so there's nothing to refresh.

## {{label:approvals.show}} {#show}

Which requests to list: **{{label:approvals.tabPending}}** (default), **{{label:approvals.tabHistory}}** for everything decided, **{{label:approvals.scopeAll}}**, or a single outcome. Your choice is kept in this browser.

If new requests arrive while you're looking at history, opening the workspace again switches back to Pending.

## Search {#search}

Finds requests by access key, tool, title or reason, among all the requests the Show choice covers.

## The list {#list}

Each row shows the request and its status. More columns, such as the access key, tool and times, are hidden; show them from the column header's menu. Newest requests are first.

Use the arrow keys to move through the list; Review follows. A request someone is approving right now shows as **{{label:approvals.processing}}**.

## {{label:approvals.selectMultiple}} {#select-multiple}

Approves several pending requests at once. Select **{{label:approvals.selectMultiple}}** on the toolbar, tick the requests, then select the Approve button that counts them. It's available while at least two pending requests are listed.

Phoenix approves them one at a time, newest first, and stops at the first one that doesn't succeed. The rest stay pending and ticked. A batch holds at most 50 requests, so selecting all ticks the first 50. Expired requests can't be ticked. **{{label:approvals.cancelSelection}}** leaves without approving.

Use it only for requests you already know well; each one still runs exactly as Review shows it.

## {{label:common.loadMore}} {#load-more}

Loads the next 50 requests.

window.capabilities.body:
Capabilities decide which tools the key may use. Each one is Deny, Allow or Confirm, and a new key starts with every capability set to Deny.

Capabilities and the [{{label:accessKeys.permissionsTree}}](guide:window.permissions) work together. Capabilities decide what kind of thing the agent may do; the tree decides which devices and entities it can do it to.

## Deny, Allow and Confirm {#modes}

- **{{label:caps.modeDeny}}**: the agent can't use these tools. They aren't offered to it.
- **{{label:caps.modeAllow}}**: the agent uses them freely.
- **{{label:caps.modeConfirm}}**: changes wait in [Approvals](guide:workspace.approvals) until you decide. Reads covered by the same capability run straight away.

Reads and Everyday Actions are Deny or Allow only; their Confirm button is greyed out.

A change applies straight away. Loosening a capability lets the agent carry on. Tightening one refuses the agent's requests already in progress. When you approve a request, Phoenix checks the capability again, and the request fails if it's now Deny.

Related: [Inline approval wait](guide:window.keySettings#inline-wait), [What pass-through opens](guide:topic.passThrough)

## {{label:caps.tierRead}} {#reads}

### {{label:caps.cap_config_read.label}} {#config-read}

Reads Home Assistant's configuration, Energy settings, solar forecast, blueprints and MESA profiles. Pass-through allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_camera_read.label}} {#camera-read}

Takes still images from cameras the key can reach. No persona allows it, and pass-through doesn't either.

### {{label:caps.cap_template_render.label}} {#template-render}

Renders Home Assistant templates. Pass-through allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_log_read.label}} {#log-read}

Reads the system log, the logbook, notifications and integration log levels. Pass-through doesn't allow it.

### {{label:caps.cap_search.label}} {#search}

Searches entities and describes areas, entities, relationships and available actions, including dry runs. Every persona and pass-through allow it.

### {{label:caps.cap_registry_read.label}} {#registry-read}

Lists areas, floors, zones, devices, automations, scripts, scenes, helpers and labels. Every persona and pass-through allow it.

### {{label:caps.cap_traces.label}} {#traces}

Reads automation traces. Pass-through allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_diagnostics.label}} {#diagnostics}

Reads system health, repairs, configuration checks, radio networks and the ESPHome overview, and validates automations and scripts. With {{label:caps.cap_log_read.label}}, it also reads the fault log, captured debug records and Phoenix's own diagnostics. Pass-through allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_filesystem.label}} {#filesystem}

Reads files in the www, themes and custom_templates folders. Pass-through doesn't allow it.

## {{label:caps.tierEveryday}} {#everyday}

### {{label:caps.cap_broadcast.label}} {#broadcast}

Broadcasts a message through Assist satellites, starts conversations on them and plays announcements and spoken text on speakers, on top of Write on the speaker. Ordinary playback needs only Write. Pass-through allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_service_response.label}} {#service-response}

Returns the data an action responds with. Without it, most actions still run but return no data. Phoenix refuses actions that only return data, such as weather forecasts and Music Assistant library searches. Pass-through allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_esphome_yaml.label}} {#esphome-yaml}

Reads and searches ESPHome device configuration. With the ESPHome Device Builder, it also validates, compiles and inspects devices. Pass-through doesn't allow it.

## {{label:caps.tierConfigWrite}} {#config-changes}

Pass-through never changes these; each keeps its own setting.

### {{label:caps.cap_automation_write.label}} {#automation-write}

Reads, creates, edits and deletes automations. Adding a call that runs code or reaches the host, a restart or update, a templated action name, or a blueprint always needs your approval, even on Allow.

### {{label:caps.cap_script_write.label}} {#script-write}

Reads, creates, edits and deletes scripts. The approval rule for automations applies here too.

### {{label:caps.cap_blueprint_write.label}} {#blueprint-write}

Creates, edits and deletes blueprints. Writing a blueprint also needs the matching automations or scripts capability: Deny on either refuses it, and Confirm on either holds it for approval. The approval rule for automations applies here too.

### {{label:caps.cap_scene_write.label}} {#scene-write}

Reads, creates, edits and deletes scenes.

### {{label:caps.cap_helper_write.label}} {#helper-write}

Creates, edits and deletes helpers, and changes their settings.

### {{label:caps.cap_calendar_write.label}} {#calendar-write}

Creates, changes and deletes calendar events.

## {{label:caps.tierSystem}} {#system}

### {{label:caps.cap_physical_control.label}} {#physical-control}

Controls locks, alarm panels, covers and valves, through their own actions or any other action that reaches them. Raw device commands such as `zwave_js.set_value` always need it, and so do ESPHome actions on a device with one of these. The agent also needs W on them in the {{label:accessKeys.permissionsTree}}. Radio tools that change Zigbee devices need it too.

Creating or editing a scene with one of these in it needs this capability as well.

### {{label:caps.cap_notify.label}} {#notify}

Sends notifications, through its own tool, a notify action or any other action that sends through a notify entity.

### {{label:caps.cap_restart.label}} {#restart}

Restarts or stops Home Assistant and installs updates. Every update needs your approval, even on Allow. ESPHome firmware uses its own capability instead.

### {{label:caps.cap_log_control.label}} {#log-control}

Changes an integration's log level. It also needs {{label:caps.cap_log_read.label}}. No persona allows it.

### {{label:caps.cap_integration_write.label}} {#integration-write}

Adds, changes, enables, disables, reloads and removes integrations, and removes devices.

### {{label:caps.cap_integration_reconfigure.label}} {#integration-reconfigure}

Reconfigures an integration.

### {{label:caps.cap_lovelace_write.label}} {#lovelace-write}

Reads and changes dashboards. Without it, the agent can't read dashboards either.

### {{label:caps.cap_registry_write.label}} {#registry-write}

Renames entities, changes their area, device, labels and categories, and deletes them. An entity's ID can change within its domain. Purging an entity's recorded history with `recorder.purge_entities` needs this capability too.

### {{label:caps.cap_radio_write.label}} {#radio-write}

Manages Zigbee networks. Scanning the network also needs {{label:caps.cap_diagnostics.label}}; changing devices, bindings and groups also needs {{label:caps.cap_physical_control.label}}.

### {{label:caps.cap_energy_write.label}} {#energy-write}

Changes the Energy dashboard's settings.

### {{label:caps.cap_esphome_flash.label}} {#esphome-flash}

Installs firmware on ESPHome devices, through the ESPHome Device Builder. It also needs {{label:caps.cap_esphome_yaml.label}}. Only the ESPHome Devices persona allows it, with your approval. `update.install` on an ESPHome device needs this capability as well.

### {{label:caps.cap_yaml_edit.label}} {#yaml-edit}

Reads Home Assistant's YAML configuration files and runs its reload actions. It also adds file and line numbers to configuration checks.

## {{label:caps.tierIrreversible}} {#high-risk}

### {{label:caps.cap_backup.label}} {#backup}

Lists and creates backups.

### {{label:caps.cap_dashboard_resources.label}} {#dashboard-resources}

Adds and changes dashboard resources, the JavaScript files that custom cards load. Every change needs your approval, so Allow isn't available. Files must come from `/local/` or `/hacsfiles/`, and Phoenix MCP changes only the resources it added itself.

window.changes.body:
Every change Phoenix recorded, newest first. Choose one to compare it in [{{label:workspace.comparison}}](guide:window.comparison). The list updates itself as agents make changes.

## {{label:logs.filters}} {#filters}

**{{label:logs.filters}}** on the toolbar shows the filters: a type, an action (create, edit, delete or rollback), and a search box. The filters look through every recorded change, not only the rows on screen, and search matches item, type, action, who made it and summary.

Changing a filter or the search clears the chosen change. Closing the filters stops them from applying; they come back as they were when you reopen them. **{{label:logs.clearFilters}}** resets all three.

## The list {#list}

Each row shows when the change was made, the item, the action, its type, and who made it: the access key, or **{{label:changes.byAdmin}}** for a restore from this panel.

Sort, move and hide columns from the column header's menu, and drag a header's edge to resize one. The count under the list includes changes not loaded yet.

## {{label:common.loadMore}} {#load-more}

Loads the next 100 changes.

window.comparison.body:
The chosen change, as the item looked before and after it. The line under the title says what the change was, its type, who made it and when, followed by a short summary.

## Stepping through changes {#step}

**{{label:workspace.previousRecord}}** and **{{label:workspace.nextRecord}}** move through the list you chose the change from: the Changes list, or Item History.

## The views {#views}

Before and after are compared line by line, removed lines in red and added lines in green. Switch between **{{label:changes.lineViewShort}}** and **{{label:changes.codeEditorShort}}**, and between **{{label:changes.stackedShort}}** and **{{label:changes.sideBySideShort}}**.

A dashboard change can also be shown as a **{{label:common.preview}}** that draws the dashboard, before or after. These choices are kept in this browser and shared with Approvals.

## {{label:changes.restore}} {#restore}

Puts the item back as it was before this change (**{{label:changes.restoreBefore}}**) or right after it (**{{label:changes.restoreAfter}}**). A restore runs with your authority as an administrator, so it needs no approval.

A greyed-out choice can't work here, and the reason shows under it. For example, a side that doesn't exist, the After of the item's newest change, an entity that was deleted, or a version holding YAML tags such as !secret. A restore can still be refused after you confirm if it no longer fits the item as it is now.

Related: [What a restore can put back](guide:topic.configurationHistory#restore)

### Confirming {#confirm}

Choosing a side asks you to confirm in place. Select **{{label:changes.confirmRestoreButton}}**, or **{{label:changes.cancelRestore}}** to back out.

If the item has a newer change by the time you confirm, Phoenix restores nothing and tells you, so check the newer change first. The server checks this too, so a change that lands at the last moment is caught as well.

### After a restore {#after-restore}

The restore is recorded as a new change, a rollback, and becomes the chosen change. To undo it, restore that rollback's Before.

window.conversationLog.body:
What agents said and did, newest first. Agent Chat turns from one chat show as one conversation.

Related: [What the Logs Record](guide:topic.logRecords#conversation-sources)

## Turning recording on {#recording}

The log is empty until you turn on **{{label:conversations.enable}}** in [{{label:workspace.logsNotifications}}](guide:window.settingsLogs#conversation-log). Select **{{label:logs.logSettings}}** on the toolbar to open it here. Recording is off by default.

## The list {#list}

Each row shows the first thing asked, the outcome, where it came from, and how many turns, tool calls and changes it holds. Choose a row to read it in [{{label:workspace.logDetails}}](guide:window.logDetails).

A row that shows a tool's name instead of a prompt is a tool call from an MCP client, Assist or Home Companion. They don't pass on what the person said.

The list shows 25 conversations; select **{{label:common.loadMore}}** for more. Sorting by anything other than newest first sorts only the rows loaded so far.

## {{label:logs.filters}} {#filters}

Narrow the list by source, outcome and date. The search finds any recorded text: what was said, tool names and arguments, and results. A match anywhere in a conversation shows the whole conversation.

Hiding the filters stops them applying until you show them again.

## Live updates {#updates}

The list updates every 5 seconds while it's shown, and so does an open conversation in Log Details. **{{label:logs.pauseUpdates}}** holds it still; while paused, **{{label:common.refresh}}** loads the newest once.

## {{label:conversations.clear}} {#clear}

Deletes every recorded conversation, whatever the filters show. Confirm in place to go ahead. This can't be undone, and the audit log records that you did it.

window.keySettings.body:
More settings for the selected key: guidance for its agents, how its tools are offered, how long requests wait for approval, and rate limits.

## {{label:accessKeys.cardGuidance}} {#guidance}

Notes Phoenix adds to the instructions of every agent that uses this key, such as what rooms and devices are called or who uses the agent. Up to 2,000 characters. Select **Save** to keep them, or **{{label:accessKeys.notesDiscard}}**.

Guidance is advice. It never grants access, and an agent may ignore it. Agents and their model providers receive it, so never put passwords or other secrets here.

When you change it, connected agents get the new guidance with their next tool result. Phoenix hides entity IDs the key can't see, and removes text that looks like a secret.

## {{label:accessKeys.cardAgentBehavior}} {#agent-behavior}

### {{label:accessKeys.announceAllLabel}} {#announce-all}

Offers the agent every Phoenix tool, even ones this key can't use. Calls to those tools are still refused. Off by default; use it for troubleshooting only, since it costs the model more and causes failed calls.

### {{label:accessKeys.inlineWaitLabel}} {#inline-wait}

How long a request that needs approval waits for your decision before the app gets a "pending" answer: Off (default), or 30 to 180 seconds. With Off, the app hears straight away that the request is waiting in Approvals.

This applies to external apps only. Agent Chat waits up to 15 minutes for your decision. The Voice Agent, Assist and AI Task report that the change is waiting and carry on. If the key already has a request waiting, a new one doesn't wait.

### {{label:accessKeys.assistExposureLabel}} {#assist-exposure}

Limits a pass-through key to the entities exposed to Assist in Home Assistant. Off by default. It appears only for pass-through keys; scoped keys use the permissions tree instead.

## {{label:accessKeys.cardRateLimiting}} {#rate-limiting}

Rate limits apply to external apps only. Agent Chat, the Voice Agent, Assist and AI Task are never limited. Each field saves when you leave it.

### {{label:accessKeys.rateRequestsLabel}} {#rate-requests}

The maximum requests the key can make in any minute. Set to 0 to turn off rate limiting. New keys start at 60.

### {{label:accessKeys.rateBurstLabel}} {#rate-burst}

The maximum requests in any one second. Set to 0 for no per-second limit. New keys start at 10. Greyed out while requests per minute is 0.

window.logDetails.body:
The record you chose in the [{{label:shell.tabAudit}}](guide:window.auditLog) or the [{{label:conversations.title}}](guide:window.conversationLog), in full.

## Stepping through records {#step}

**{{label:workspace.previousRecord}}** and **{{label:workspace.nextRecord}}** move through the log in the order it showed when you chose the record. Entries that arrive later aren't included until you choose a record again.

## An audit entry {#audit-entry}

Every field of the entry, including its **{{label:audit.rowRequestId}}**. A tool call also shows its **{{label:audit.rowPayload}}**: the tool's name and arguments, with passwords, tokens, keys and alarm or lock codes hidden. Long payloads are cut short.

If the access key was renamed since, its name then shows in brackets after the current one.

## A conversation {#conversation}

The heading sums up the conversation: where it came from, when, and how many turns, tool calls and changes it holds. Its turns follow, oldest first, 25 at a time.

Each turn shows what was asked, each tool call with its result, changes with their before and after, and the reply. Long text is folded. **{{label:conversations.copy}}** copies a block of text. **{{label:conversations.details}}** and **{{label:conversations.technical}}** show what was recorded exactly.

window.mesaAdvisory.body:
Things MESA noticed while checking your profiles and automations. The window appears only while it has something to show. None of it changes how MESA treats an action.

## Partly inspected automations {#inspection}

Automations that use templates or blueprints, so MESA couldn't read every entity they touch. Open **{{label:mesa.viewAutomationDetails}}** to list them. You don't need to do anything.

## Trigger findings {#validation}

An entity whose profile says **{{label:mesa.fieldTriggersAutomations}}** is {{label:mesa.trigNone}}, but an automation uses it. In a trigger it's an error, in a condition a warning. Each finding says what to change, in the profile or in the automation.

Related: [{{label:mesa.fieldTriggersAutomations}}](guide:window.mesaEditor#triggers-automations)

## Orphaned profiles {#orphans}

Profiles whose entity, device, area or integration no longer exists, such as after you remove a device. An integration counts as gone when it has no entities and isn't loaded. Choose one to open it in [{{label:workspace.profileEditor}}](guide:window.mesaEditor), where you can delete it. Phoenix never deletes them for you.

## {{label:mesa.dismiss}} {#dismiss}

Hides one group of findings in this browser. It comes back when a new finding joins the group. Choose **{{label:mesa.restoreNotices}}** in the MESA Actions menu to see dismissed findings again.

## When findings update {#refresh}

MESA checks again when Home Assistant starts, when automations reload, and when you save, delete or import a profile here, so a finding you've fixed goes away. **{{label:mesa.rescan}}** checks again straight away.

window.persona.body:
A persona sets all of the key's capabilities at once, to suit a kind of agent. Choose one, then adjust single capabilities in [{{label:accessKeys.cardCapabilities}}](guide:window.capabilities) if you need to. Your choice applies straight away.

A persona decides which tools the agent may use, not which devices it can reach. That's the [{{label:accessKeys.permissionsTree}}](guide:window.permissions). The one exception is Home Companion.

Personas don't stop an agent from controlling devices. With every persona except Home Companion, the agent can control anything you set to W in the tree. Only locks, alarm panels, covers and valves also need [{{label:caps.cap_physical_control.label}}](guide:window.capabilities#physical-control).

With any [{{label:settings.colorThemeLabel}}](guide:window.settingsGeneral#color-theme) except {{label:settings.colorTheme.spartan}}, three personas have their own color: {{label:personas.home_companion.label}} gold, {{label:personas.new_user.label}} green and {{label:personas.custom.label}} blue.

## {{label:personas.read_only.label}} {#read-only}

Reads only: configuration, templates, system logs, search, the registry, automation traces, diagnostics and service response data. It changes no configuration, but it can still control anything set to W in the tree.

## {{label:personas.voice_assistant.label}} {#voice-assistant}

Reads configuration, templates, search and the registry, gets service response data, and can broadcast. Notifications and physical control need your approval.

## {{label:personas.dashboard_designer.label}} {#dashboard-designer}

Reads configuration, templates, search, the registry and theme and card files, and manages dashboards. Changes to the Energy dashboard and to dashboard resources need your approval.

## {{label:personas.maintenance.label}} {#maintenance}

Reads configuration, templates, logs, search, the registry, traces and diagnostics, and creates backups. Restarts, entity registry changes and radio management need your approval.

## {{label:personas.automation_builder.label}} {#automation-builder}

All the usual reads, and manages automations, scripts, blueprints, scenes, helpers and calendar events. It can also broadcast. Notifications, physical control and restarts need your approval.

## {{label:personas.power_user.label}} {#power-user}

Everything Automation Builder does, and restarts without asking. Integrations, dashboards, the entity registry, radios, the Energy dashboard, dashboard resources and backups need your approval.

## {{label:personas.home_admin.label}} {#home-admin}

Everything Power User does, plus reading theme and card files and ESPHome device YAML. Restarts and YAML configuration need your approval too. It doesn't allow camera images, integration log levels or ESPHome firmware flashing.

## {{label:personas.new_user.label}} {#new-user}

Reads configuration, templates, search and the registry, and gets service response data. Physical control needs your approval. The setup wizard starts with this persona.

## {{label:personas.esphome.label}} {#esphome}

Reads configuration, logs, search, the registry, diagnostics and ESPHome device YAML. Flashing firmware needs your approval; this is the only persona that allows it.

## {{label:personas.home_companion.label}} {#home-companion}

For a household helper. The agent gets a single tool that passes requests to Phoenix's own model, and reaches only household devices, whatever the tree grants. Physical control needs your approval.

In Agent Chat it uses the account chosen there; everywhere else it uses the Voice Agent's account and model. It can't use pass-through or announce all tools: choosing it turns both off, and you're told when it does. Changing a capability doesn't turn it into Custom.

## {{label:personas.custom.label}} {#custom}

Any mix of capabilities. Change a single capability and the persona becomes Custom, or the persona whose settings now match.

Choosing Custom again brings back the key's earlier custom settings, if it was Custom when you switched away and you haven't reloaded the page.

window.presets.body:
A preset is a saved setup for this key. Switch between presets to change the key quickly, for example a careful everyday preset and a broader one for maintenance. This window appears when **{{label:settings.presetsLabel}}** is on in [{{label:workspace.general}}](guide:window.settingsGeneral#presets).

A preset holds the key's persona, capabilities, permissions tree, pass-through, Assist exposure, tool announcement, approval wait and rate limits. It doesn't hold the key's name or guidance.

Each key can have up to 8 presets. The one in use is marked **{{label:accessKeys.presetActive}}**. **{{label:accessKeys.presetModified}}** means the key has changed since that preset was last saved.

## {{label:accessKeys.presetSwitchTo}} {#switch-to}

Saves the key's current settings into the active preset, then applies the one you chose. A list of what changes shows first, for you to confirm. If the preset turns on pass-through, confirm that too.

Switching tells connected agents that their tools changed, and restarts the key's rate limit count.

## {{label:accessKeys.presetRevertToSaved}} {#revert}

Throws away the changes made since the active preset was saved, after you confirm.

## {{label:accessKeys.presetSaveCurrent}} {#save-current}

Saves the key's current settings as a new preset and makes it the active one. Type a name of up to 40 characters first. The preset you were on keeps its last saved state.

## Renaming and deleting {#rename-delete}

Edit a preset's name in place; press Enter to save or Esc to cancel. **{{label:accessKeys.delete}}** removes a preset after you confirm. You can't delete the active preset.

window.requestDetails.body:
The technical record of the request chosen in Review. It opens with the workspace, under Review; if you close it, reopen it from **{{label:workspace.options}}**.

Each part folds, and stays folded or open for the next request you choose.

## {{label:approvals.colRequest}} {#request}

The access key, tool, capability, when the request was made and expires or was decided, and its status. A decided request also shows the reason it ended.

## {{label:approvals.detailTabArgs}} {#raw-args}

The arguments the agent sent. Values that look like secrets are hidden, and so are Phoenix's own internal arguments. Folded by default.

## {{label:approvals.detailTabResult}} {#result}

What the action returned, for a decided request, including any error.

## After an hour {#pruned}

An hour after a request is decided, Phoenix clears its arguments, change and result. The request stays in history with its outcome, but Review and these sections have nothing left to show.

window.settingsHome.body:
Connect Phoenix to Home Assistant's own AI features. Each tab sets up one feature, and in each one an access key decides what Phoenix may do.

- {{label:settings.voiceCard}}: Phoenix answers Home Assistant's voice and Assist conversations, using your account and model.
- {{label:settings.aiTaskCard}}: Phoenix runs Home Assistant's AI Task actions, using your account and model.
- {{label:settings.assistCard}}: another integration's model uses Phoenix's tools. Phoenix runs no model of its own.

Related: [Which one to use](guide:topic.homeAssistantFeatures)

## {{label:settings.voiceCard}} {#voice-agent}

Makes Phoenix a Home Assistant conversation agent named Phoenix MCP. Set the access key, account and model first: the agent appears only once all three are set.

### {{label:settings.voiceEnableLabel}} {#voice-enable}

Turns the agent on. Off by default. Turning it off also removes the Assist assistant Phoenix created.

While the **{{label:settings.killSwitchCard}}** is on, the agent stays, but only replies that it's unavailable.

### {{label:settings.voiceAssistSetup}} {#voice-assistant}

Creates a Home Assistant assistant named Phoenix MCP that uses this agent, with Home Assistant's default speech-to-text and text-to-speech. Available once the agent is fully set up.

Turning it off deletes the assistant. Home Assistant always keeps at least one assistant, so if it's the only one, it stays, but Phoenix stops managing it.

Phoenix also removes its assistant if you clear the access key, account or model, or revoke the key. Home Assistant keeps it while it is its only assistant, and Phoenix tells you so. Remove it in Home Assistant after adding another.

### {{label:settings.voicePreferredLabel}} {#voice-preferred}

Makes Phoenix's assistant the one Home Assistant uses by default. Turning it off hands that role to the first assistant in Home Assistant's list, which may not be the one you used before.

### {{label:settings.accessKey}} {#voice-access-key}

The access key that decides what the agent may do. You can only choose an active key. If the key is revoked or expires, it's cleared here.

A Home Companion key hands conversations to the companion, and hides **{{label:routing.enableVoice}}**.

### {{label:settings.providerAccount}} {#voice-provider}

The account whose model answers. Decision provider accounts aren't listed. Add an account in [{{label:workspace.aiProviders}}](guide:window.settingsProviders) first.

### {{label:settings.model}} {#voice-model}

The model that answers. Choose the account first. If the provider retires the model, Phoenix switches to its successor.

### {{label:routing.enableVoice}} {#voice-commands}

Carries out simple commands without your AI model, as in Agent Chat. Off by default.

Related: [How a command is routed](guide:topic.commandRouting)

### {{label:settings.conversationStyle}} {#voice-style}

How replies sound, with the same choices as [Agent Chat](guide:window.settingsChat#chat-style). Default: {{label:settings.styleDirect}}.

### {{label:settings.detailLevel}} {#voice-detail}

How much replies say. Default: {{label:settings.detailConcise}}.

### {{label:settings.homeFocused}} {#voice-home-focused}

Asks the model to stick to your home. This is a preference, not a security control.

### {{label:routingStats.title}} {#voice-statistics}

**{{label:routingStats.show}}** opens a window that counts how voice commands were handled.

Related: [Reading the statistics](guide:topic.routingStatistics)

## {{label:settings.aiTaskCard}} {#ai-task}

Gives Home Assistant an AI Task entity named Phoenix MCP AI Task, which automations and scripts use to generate text or data. It acts for the household, within its access key. If Home Assistant says who started a task, the task has only that person's rights.

### {{label:settings.aiTaskEnableLabel}} {#ai-task-enable}

Turns the entity on. Off by default. The entity exists only while the access key, account and model are all set.

If you clear any of them, Phoenix removes the entity, and any changes you made to it in Home Assistant are lost.

### {{label:settings.aiTaskDefaultLabel}} {#ai-task-default}

Makes Phoenix's entity Home Assistant's default AI Task. This replaces any current default straight away, without asking. Quick Help shows which one it replaces.

Turning it off leaves Home Assistant with no default. The previous default isn't restored.

### {{label:settings.accessKey}} {#ai-task-access-key}

The access key that decides what AI Task may do. It works the same as the [Voice Agent's](guide:#voice-access-key).

### {{label:settings.providerAccount}} {#ai-task-provider}

The account whose model runs the tasks.

### {{label:settings.model}} {#ai-task-model}

The model that runs the tasks.

### {{label:settings.conversationStyle}} {#ai-task-style}

How text results sound. Default: {{label:settings.styleDirect}}.

### {{label:settings.detailLevel}} {#ai-task-detail}

How much text results say. Default: {{label:settings.detailBalanced}}. Tasks that ask for structured data use their own format instead.

## {{label:settings.aiDecisionsHeading}} {#ai-decisions}

Gives Home Assistant a second entity, Phoenix MCP Decisions, for the Evaluate action. Automations and scripts use it to ask yes-or-no, choice and rating questions about text or data they pass in. Your decision provider answers, and each answer comes with probabilities.

This section shows only on Home Assistant 2026.11 or later, while a decision provider is in use. To add or choose one, go to [{{label:workspace.aiProviders}}](guide:window.settingsProviders#decision-provider).

The questions and the data passed in go to your decision provider, and every request sent counts towards your [request limit](guide:window.settingsProviders#decision-request-limit). An automation that acts on an answer runs as Home Assistant, outside Phoenix's checks.

A person who starts an Evaluate action needs control of the Decisions entity, even when the action names no entity. An automation no person started doesn't. Each call and refusal goes in the [audit log](guide:topic.logRecords#audit-entries).

### {{label:settings.aiDecisionsEnableLabel}} {#ai-decisions-enable}

Turns the entity on. Off by default. The entity exists only while this is on and an access key is chosen.

Revoking the access key or removing the decision provider turns this off and removes the entity, along with any changes you made to it in Home Assistant.

### {{label:settings.aiDecisionsDefaultLabel}} {#ai-decisions-default}

Makes this Home Assistant's default for Evaluate actions that name no entity. It replaces any current default straight away.

### {{label:settings.accessKey}} {#ai-decisions-access-key}

The access key that lets Evaluate actions run. It's separate from the AI Task's key. It must be active, with **{{label:caps.cap_service_response.label}}** set to {{label:caps.modeAllow}}, which a pass-through key already counts as.

Entity permissions and MESA don't apply here: the action has no target entity and changes nothing.

## {{label:settings.assistCard}} {#assist-tool-provider}

Lends Phoenix's tools to a model that runs elsewhere, such as a conversation agent set to Control Home Assistant with Phoenix MCP (scoped). Phoenix runs no model of its own here.

### {{label:settings.assistBoundAccessKey}} {#assist-access-key}

The access key whose tools the other model gets. With **{{label:settings.assistNotBound}}** (default), no tools are offered. The same happens while the **{{label:settings.killSwitchCard}}** is on.

Changing the key refuses tool calls from earlier turns, so a conversation can't keep the old key's rights. Home Assistant's MCP Server uses the same key, without the key's rate limit.

Related: [Through Home Assistant's MCP Server](guide:topic.connecting#ha-mcp-server)

window.settingsLogs.body:
Choose what Phoenix records, how long it keeps it, and when Home Assistant notifies you. This window also opens from **{{label:logs.logSettings}}** in the {{label:workspace.logs}} workspace.

## {{label:shell.tabAudit}} {#audit-log}

The audit log records what agents do through Phoenix and what administrators change, with each outcome.

Related: [What the logs record](guide:topic.logRecords)

### {{label:settings.logEnableLabel}} {#log-enable}

Records audit entries and conversations. Turn it off to stop all recording; the other logging settings are then greyed out. On by default.

Phoenix's sensors keep counting, and notifications still arrive.

### {{label:settings.logAllowedLabel}} {#log-allowed}

Records requests that were carried out. On by default.

### {{label:settings.logDeniedLabel}} {#log-denied}

Records requests that were refused, malformed, not supported, or named something that doesn't exist. MESA blocks count here too. On by default.

Requests waiting for approval, your approval decisions, and requests cut short while a change may have run are always recorded.

### {{label:settings.logRateLimitedLabel}} {#log-rate-limited}

Records requests turned away because a key sent too many. On by default.

These three switches affect new entries only. Existing entries stay.

### {{label:settings.flushIntervalLabel}} {#log-flush}

How often the audit log is saved to disk: every 5, 10, 15 (default), 30 or 60 minutes, or never. It's also saved when Home Assistant stops or Phoenix reloads.

### {{label:settings.maxLogEntriesLabel}} {#log-max-entries}

How many entries the audit log keeps: 100, 1,000, 5,000 or 10,000 (default). Lowering it removes the oldest entries straight away. The copy on disk shrinks at the next save.

## {{label:conversations.title}} {#conversation-log}

**{{label:conversations.enable}}** keeps a record of what agents say and do: Agent Chat and voice conversations, AI Task, and the tool calls of MCP clients, Assist and Home Companion. Review it in the {{label:workspace.logs}} workspace. Off by default. Turning it off keeps what was already recorded.

Three limits control how much is kept. Each one saves a moment after you change it. When the log goes over a limit, the oldest conversations are removed first.

### {{label:conversations.limit.entries}} {#conversation-entries}

How many conversations to keep: 1 to 2,000 (default 100).

### {{label:conversations.limit.days}} {#conversation-days}

How many days to keep a conversation: 1 to 365 (default 7).

### {{label:conversations.limit.mb}} {#conversation-size}

How much space the log can use: 1 to 250 MB (default 25). The space used now is shown below the limits. A large log takes longer to load when Home Assistant starts, and can use about twice this size in memory.

## {{label:settings.privacySection}} {#privacy}

Choose what the logs show about your home and the people using Phoenix.

### {{label:settings.logEntityIdsLabel}} {#log-entity-ids}

When off, the audit log's {{label:audit.rowResource}} column shows [redacted], and the Conversation Log leaves out what was said and done. On by default.

The audit log's payload then keeps only the names of a tool call's arguments, not their values. Other payload text, such as an approval's reason, can still name an entity. Conversation content already recorded is removed the next time Phoenix starts.

### {{label:settings.logClientIpLabel}} {#log-client-ip}

When off, both logs hide the caller's IP address, though an address written into a tool call's arguments stays in the audit payload. On by default. Agent Chat, voice, Assist, AI Task and Home Companion still show by name, since they aren't addresses.

## {{label:settings.haNotifications}} {#notifications}

Phoenix can send Home Assistant notifications, which appear in the Home Assistant sidebar.

### {{label:settings.notifyApprovalLabel}} {#notify-approval}

Sends a notification with a link to each request waiting for your approval. It goes away once you decide. On by default. The Approvals count on the toolbar shows either way.

### {{label:settings.notifyRateLimitLabel}} {#notify-rate-limit}

Notifies you when an access key sends requests faster than its limit allows. Off by default. Each key has one notification, updated at most once a minute.

workspace.my.body:
My Workspace gathers the windows you use most from the other workspaces. It starts empty.

Related: [Working with Windows](guide:topic.windows)

## Adding a window {#adding}

Choose **{{label:workspace.addToMy}}** in a window's menu. The window stays in its own workspace too, and both copies show the same chosen record. Filters, columns and sizes are separate in each copy.

The first window fills the workspace. Each next one goes beside or under the others, a row at a time.

## Removing a window {#removing}

Closing a window here removes it. To bring it back, add it again from its own workspace. **{{label:workspace.restoreOriginal}}** removes every window.

My Default here keeps the arrangement and the windows in it, so restoring it brings back windows you removed since.

## {{label:workspace.verticalScrolling}} {#scrolling}

Off by default, so the windows share the screen's height. Turn it on from any window's menu here to let the workspace grow taller and scroll. Each window then gets back the height it had before.

With it on, drag the border under a window to make it taller.

## Agent Chat {#chat}

Choose **{{label:workspace.addToMy}}** in Agent Chat's own menu to dock it here. While another workspace is shown, Chat floats over it, and it docks again when you come back here. If you closed it meanwhile, it stays closed; **{{label:shell.agentChat}}** in its window opens it. **{{label:agentchat.floatChat}}** undocks it, and closing it here closes Chat.

## On a phone {#phones}

Choose **{{label:workspace.addToMy}}** in a window's menu to add it here, and close it here to remove it. Agent Chat can't be added on a phone. A phone keeps its own My Workspace, apart from the one on a computer.
